  $n_Lokay_guard_web_DocsText$ = this;
  this.a3N = new $j_internal$002d767013ce0ee0f6d7a07587912eba3104cfaabc15.$c_sci_$colon$colon($j_internal$002d767013ce0ee0f6d7a07587912eba3104cfaabc15.$ct_T2__O__O__(new $j_internal$002d767013ce0ee0f6d7a07587912eba3104cfaabc15.$c_T2(), "about", "# About\n\nokay-guard is written in Scala 3 and Scala.js on [okay-effects](https://okay-effects.github.io/), a family of\nalgebraic effect libraries.\n\nThe guard is a program over its own effects: the wire, the journal, the policy, a person, the clock. Every place it\nruns only handles those effects:\n\n- your browser (this studio);\n- a Cloudflare Worker that passes messages between your agent and the page;\n- your machine, on Node or the JVM.\n\nA call waiting for you is the program's continuation, kept as data until you answer.\n\nThe policy language is [okay-proof](https://github.com/okay-effects/okay-proof)'s okay-policy. Every decision by a\nrule is a theorem of its kernel, with a certificate anyone can check again.\n\n## Built on okay-effects\n\nokay-effects makes what a program does explicit and controllable:\n\n- **types** say what a program may do;\n- **handlers** say how it is done;\n- **interceptors** put each action through a point of control, and audit it.\n\nokay-guard is that idea applied to AI agents: every action an agent takes goes through a policy, a person if needed,\nand a journal.\n\n- The site: [okay-effects.github.io](https://okay-effects.github.io/)\n- The code: [github.com/okay-effects](https://github.com/okay-effects)\n\n## Need more?\n\nNeed okay-guard fitted to your services, a policy written for your domain, or effects in your own Scala or JVM\nsystem? [Need more?](https://okay-effects.github.io/#author) Contact the author.\n"), new $j_internal$002d767013ce0ee0f6d7a07587912eba3104cfaabc15.$c_sci_$colon$colon($j_internal$002d767013ce0ee0f6d7a07587912eba3104cfaabc15.$ct_T2__O__O__(new $j_internal$002d767013ce0ee0f6d7a07587912eba3104cfaabc15.$c_T2(), "journal", "# The journal\n\nEvery call your agent makes is recorded, with what the policy decided and why. The journal is kept where the studio\nis: in your browser on the site, in the workspace's `journal/` on your machine.\n\n## A record\n\nOne record a call, when the call has ended:\n\n- **the action:** the service, the operation, who called it, and its arguments, with secrets masked;\n- **the policy:** its name and the hash of its text. The text itself is kept beside the journal, so a record can be\n  read against the policy that decided it;\n- **the decision and why:**\n  - by a rule: the rule and the values it met (`amount = 30000`), and the rules tried before it;\n  - by default: the default, and why each rule did not apply;\n  - by the policy language: its reasons, the facts it used and the certificate of the theorem;\n- **a person's verdict:** approved, refused or timed out; how long it took; the arguments if changed;\n- **the outcome:** what the service answered, and how long it took;\n- **the webhooks** it was sent to.\n\nEach record carries the hash of the one before. A record changed or taken out breaks the chain, and the check says\nwhere.\n\n## In the studio\n\n**Log** shows every message as it went. Its journal section has:\n\n- **Download (JSON lines)**: one record a line, for scripts and `jq`;\n- **Download (CSV)**: a row a call, for a spreadsheet;\n- **Check the chain**: is the journal whole?\n\n**Tests \u2192 Replay the journal** decides every recorded call again by the policy as it is now.\n\n## On your machine\n\nThe journal is `~/.okay-guard/journal/<day>.jsonl`, a file a day; the policies' texts are in\n`journal/policies/`. `--keep 90` removes the days older than 90.\n\n```\nokay-guard journal --since 7d                       # the records, a line each\nokay-guard journal --decision block --agent shop    # only some\nokay-guard journal --csv > journal.csv              # for a spreadsheet; --json for scripts\nokay-guard journal --verify                         # the chain whole, every policy found\nokay-guard journal replay --policy new.policy       # what the new policy would change; exit 1 if anything\n```\n\n## Webhooks\n\nA policy may send a call's record to a webhook: on a rule (`notify security`), or on an outcome\n(`notify ops on refused create_refund`). See [webhooks](policy.md#webhooks). The record is sent as JSON, with a\n`text` line on top that a Slack channel shows as it is.\n"), new $j_internal$002d767013ce0ee0f6d7a07587912eba3104cfaabc15.$c_sci_$colon$colon($j_internal$002d767013ce0ee0f6d7a07587912eba3104cfaabc15.$ct_T2__O__O__(new $j_internal$002d767013ce0ee0f6d7a07587912eba3104cfaabc15.$c_T2(), "machine", "# On your machine\n\nThe same studio and the same policy run on your machine. The page opens at `127.0.0.1` with a token of its own, and\nevery server is reachable: local commands, and servers a browser may not call.\n\n## Install\n\n- **Node 18+:** download [okay-guard.zip](https://okay-effects.github.io/okay-guard/download/okay-guard.zip), unzip\n  it, and run `node okay-guard.js --open`.\n- **Java 17+:** download [okay-guard.jar](https://okay-effects.github.io/okay-guard/download/okay-guard.jar) and run\n  `java -jar okay-guard.jar --open`.\n\nMade something on the site first? There, **Tools \u2192 Export a copy**; here, **Tools \u2192 Import a copy**. The agents, their\nservers and their policies come along.\n\n## Connect your agent\n\nYour agent connects to the address the studio shows, `http://127.0.0.1:\u2026/m/\u2026/mcp`. Or put okay-guard where a server's\ncommand was, in any MCP client over stdio; the policy is a file:\n\n```\n{ \"mcpServers\": { \"stripe\": { \"command\": \"node\",\n    \"args\": [\"/path/to/okay-guard.js\", \"--rules\", \"/path/to/stripe.policy\", \"--open\",\n             \"--\", \"npx\", \"-y\", \"@stripe/mcp\", \"--api-key=${STRIPE_KEY}\"] } } }\n```\n\nOn Java, use `\"command\": \"java\", \"args\": [\"-jar\", \"/path/to/okay-guard.jar\", \u2026]`.\n\n## Servers\n\nIn **Tools**:\n\n- an MCP server by its URL;\n- an MCP server by its command, run by the machine; `${NAME}` in it is the variable `NAME` of the environment, so a\n  key stays out of the files;\n- a REST API by its OpenAPI, with a header for its key if it needs one.\n\n## Commands\n\n| command | what it does |\n|---|---|\n| `okay-guard --open` | the studio |\n| `okay-guard --lan` | the page in the local network too (a phone on the same Wi-Fi) |\n| `okay-guard check x.policy` | the policy's tests (`x.tests` beside it); exit 1 if one fails |\n| `okay-guard journal \u2026` | the [journal](journal.md): read, exported, checked, replayed |\n| `okay-guard --rules x.policy -- <server>` | an agent on stdio, its calls decided by the policy |\n| `okay-guard --keep 90` | the journal's days older than 90 removed |\n\nA policy's webhooks with `env \"VAR\"` take the address from the environment here.\n"), new $j_internal$002d767013ce0ee0f6d7a07587912eba3104cfaabc15.$c_sci_$colon$colon($j_internal$002d767013ce0ee0f6d7a07587912eba3104cfaabc15.$ct_T2__O__O__(new $j_internal$002d767013ce0ee0f6d7a07587912eba3104cfaabc15.$c_T2(), "policy", "# The policy language\n\nA policy says what an AI agent may do with its services: what goes ahead, what waits for a person, and what is never\ndone. okay-guard checks every call against it before the call reaches the service.\n\nThe language is small. It has declarations of what can be known, and rules about what is allowed. It is typed: a\nmisspelt argument or a comparison of a text with a number is an error in the editor, before anything runs. Every\ndecision is explained in the journal, and a decision by a rule is a theorem with a certificate anyone can check again.\n\nAll the examples below are real files, checked by okay-guard with their tests.\n\n## A first policy\n\nAn agent handles refunds. It may look at charges and make small refunds. A person decides on a big refund. It never\ndeletes a customer.\n\n```policy file=first.policy\n```\n\n- `policy refunds` names the policy.\n- `type User` declares who calls. Every rule may use `caller`, the agent that calls.\n- `call` declares a tool and the types of its arguments. A rule may only use the arguments its tool has.\n- `allow`, `ask`, `deny` are the rules. `when` is the condition; `because` is the reason the agent and the person are\n  told.\n\nIn the studio the policy is in **Rules**. On your machine it is a file, `refunds.policy`, with its tests in\n`refunds.tests`: `okay-guard check refunds.policy` runs them.\n\n## How a call is decided\n\nFor each call, okay-guard takes the rules whose target matches the call and checks their conditions. Then:\n\n1. If a `deny` applies, the call is **denied**.\n2. Otherwise, if an `ask` applies, a **person is asked**.\n3. Otherwise, if an `allow` applies, the call is **allowed**.\n4. Otherwise the policy's **default** decides: `default allow`, `default ask` or `default deny`. A policy with no\n   `default` denies.\n\nThe order of the rules in the text does not matter. A `deny` always wins, and an `ask` wins over an `allow`. So a\nbroad `allow` and a narrow `ask` go together: `allow github.*` lets the agent use GitHub, and\n`ask github.create_pull_request` still stops a pull request for a person.\n\n### Targets: a tool, a service, every call\n\nA rule's target is one tool (`create_refund`), every tool of a server (`github.*`), or every call (`*`). A tool written\nwith its server's name, `github.get_file`, is that server's tool only: one policy can hold rules for several servers.\n\n```policy file=servers.policy\n```\n\n### Deny everything, or allow everything\n\nWith no `default`, a policy denies what no rule allows: list what may be done. For the other way round, say\n`default allow` and list what may not. A `deny` still wins and an `ask` still asks.\n\nA tool the policy never declares, such as a server's new tool, is decided by the rules for every call (`*`, with\n`tool` its name) and by the default. Its arguments are not read, so a condition on them does not hold: declare the\ntool with `call` to decide on its arguments.\n\n```policy file=everything.policy\n```\n\n### REST APIs\n\nAn agent can call a REST API through okay-guard: add it in **Tools** by its OpenAPI. Its operations become the\nagent's tools, named by their `operationId`, and the policy decides each call before it is made.\n\n```policy file=petstore.policy\n```\n\n## Writing rules\n\n### Types\n\n| type | what it is |\n|---|---|\n| `Int` | whole numbers: amounts in cents, counts |\n| `Dec` | exact decimals, for money: `500.00` |\n| `Text` | text: `\"eur\"` |\n| `Bool` | true or false; a condition by itself: `dry_run`, `not force` |\n| `User`, `Account` | an entity type the policy declares; its values are names |\n| `type Item = { sku: Text, qty: Int }` | a record, for an argument that is a JSON object |\n| `List[Item]` | a list, for an argument that is a JSON array |\n\nAn optional argument is declared with `?`: `coupon?: Text`.\n\n### Conditions\n\n| condition | what it says |\n|---|---|\n| `amount <= 20000` | a comparison: `= != < <= > >=` |\n| `a and b`, `a or b`, `not a` | combined |\n| `currency in [\"eur\", \"usd\"]` | one of a list |\n| `path starts \"/workspace/\"` | texts: `starts`, `ends`, `contains` |\n| `amount + fee <= 100` | sums; a product by a number only |\n| `has(coupon)` | an optional argument is there |\n| `shipping.country` | a record's field |\n| `count(items) <= 20` | a list's length |\n| `every i in items: (i.qty <= 10)` | each element of a list; `some` for one of them |\n\nA missing argument makes the condition that uses it fail, so a missing argument can deny or ask but never allow.\n\n```policy file=shop.policy\n```\n\n## Facts and state\n\nA `state` is a value okay-guard keeps between calls, per `minute`, `hour` or `day`. A rule changes it with `then`,\nonly when the call goes ahead. A `count` rule decides nothing: it only changes state for every call that goes ahead.\n\nThe state is kept where the studio is: in your browser's storage on the site, in the workspace's `state/` on your\nmachine (`state/<agent>.json`; `state/outside.json` for an agent on stdio). A page reloaded or the guard started again\nkeeps the day's sums and the minute's counts; a window that has passed starts again from the initial value.\n\n```policy file=budget.policy\n```\n\nA `fact` is something the policy needs that is not in the call. `from mcp` makes okay-guard ask a tool of the agent's\nserver itself, before it decides; `at` is the path to the value in the answer. A fact that cannot be found is unknown,\nand an unknown fact never allows a call.\n\n```policy file=facts.policy\n```\n\n## Webhooks\n\nA webhook is an address okay-guard sends a message to when something happens: a Slack channel, your security\nsystem, your own service.\n\n```policy file=alerts.policy\n```\n\n- `webhook name = \"https://\u2026\"` declares an address; `webhook name = env \"VAR\"` takes it from a variable on the\n  machine, so the address (often a secret) is not in the policy.\n- `notify name` on a rule: told whenever that rule decides a call.\n- `notify name on outcome target when \u2026`: told when a call ends so. The outcomes are `allow`, `ask`, `deny`, and what\n  became of an ask: `approved`, `refused`, `timed out`.\n\nEach webhook gets the call's record from the [journal](journal.md) as JSON, with a line of text on top.\n\n## Tests\n\nA policy's tests are a file of their own, beside it. Each test is a call and the decision it must get; `given` sets\nfacts and state.\n\n```tests file=first.tests\n```\n\nIn the studio, **Tests** makes them of your agent's calls and runs them at every change of the policy. **Replay the\njournal** decides every recorded call again by the policy as it is now. On your machine:\n\n```\nokay-guard check refunds.policy                         # the tests; exit 1 if one fails\nokay-guard journal replay --policy refunds.policy       # the journal decided again; exit 1 if anything changes\n```\n\n## Errors\n\nA policy that does not compile is not used, and the error says where:\n\n```\n8:8: an unknown name amout\n11:15: Text > Int: different sorts\n```\n\n## Reference\n\n```\npolicy    ::= \"policy\" name decl*\ndecl      ::= type | record | fact | state | call | rule | count | default | webhook | notify\ntype      ::= \"type\" Name\nrecord    ::= \"type\" Name \"=\" \"{\" field (\",\" field)* \"}\"\nfact      ::= \"fact\" name \"(\" params \")\" (\":\" sort)? (\"from\" source)?\nsource    ::= name | \"mcp\" [server \".\"] tool \"(\" args \")\" \"at\" text\nstate     ::= \"state\" name \"(\" params \")\" \":\" sort (\"per\" (\"minute\" | \"hour\" | \"day\"))? \"=\" literal\ncall      ::= \"call\" [server \".\"] tool \"(\" params \")\"\nparam     ::= name \"?\"? \":\" sort\nrule      ::= (\"allow\" | \"ask\" | \"deny\") target (\"when\" cond)? (\"then\" update (\",\" update)*)? (\"because\" text)?\n              (\"notify\" name (\",\" name)*)?\ncount     ::= \"count\" target (\"when\" cond)? \"then\" update (\",\" update)*\ndefault   ::= \"default\" (\"allow\" | \"ask\" | \"deny\")\nwebhook   ::= \"webhook\" name \"=\" (text | \"env\" text)\nnotify    ::= \"notify\" name (\",\" name)* \"on\" outcome target (\"when\" cond)?\ntarget    ::= tool | server \".\" tool | server \".*\" | \"*\"\ncond      ::= cond \"and\" cond | cond \"or\" cond | \"not\" cond | \"(\" cond \")\" | atom\natom      ::= expr cmp expr | expr \"in\" \"[\" exprs \"]\" | expr (\"starts\" | \"ends\" | \"contains\") expr\n            | \"has\" \"(\" path \")\" | (\"every\" | \"some\") name \"in\" path \":\" cond | name \"(\" exprs \")\" | path\nexpr      ::= expr (\"+\" | \"-\") expr | expr \"*\" literal | \"(\" expr \")\" | literal | path | name \"(\" exprs \")\"\nupdate    ::= name \"(\" exprs \")\" (\"=\" | \"+=\" | \"-=\") expr\nsort      ::= \"Int\" | \"Dec\" | \"Text\" | \"Bool\" | Name | \"List\" \"[\" sort \"]\"\n\ntests     ::= \"tests\" \"for\" name test*\ntest      ::= \"test\" text (\"given\" given (\",\" given)*)? \"when\" name \"calls\" tool \"(\" args \")\"\n              \"expect\" (\"allow\" | \"ask\" | \"deny\") text?\n```\n\nNames of types start with a capital letter; names of facts, states and arguments with a small one. The variables of\na rule are `caller`, `tool` and the call's arguments. A tool whose name is not a plain word is written in quotes:\n`call \"list-files\"(path: Text)`. `--` starts a comment.\n\nThe language is [okay-proof](https://github.com/okay-effects/okay-proof)'s okay-policy: its full specification, the\nlogic underneath and the kernel that checks every decision are there.\n"), new $j_internal$002d767013ce0ee0f6d7a07587912eba3104cfaabc15.$c_sci_$colon$colon($j_internal$002d767013ce0ee0f6d7a07587912eba3104cfaabc15.$ct_T2__O__O__(new $j_internal$002d767013ce0ee0f6d7a07587912eba3104cfaabc15.$c_T2(), "start", "# Getting started\n\nokay-guard stands between an AI agent and the services it works with. The services are MCP servers and REST APIs.\nYou see every call the agent makes. Your policy decides what passes, what is blocked and what waits for a person, and\nyou decide where the call stands. Every call is recorded with its decision and why.\n\nThe agent is yours: Claude Code, Claude Desktop, Claude.ai, Cursor, any MCP client. The studio runs no model of its\nown and asks for no key.\n\n## The studio's places\n\n| place | what it is |\n|---|---|\n| **Tools** | how your agent connects (its address), and the servers and APIs it reaches |\n| **Rules** | the policy: its rules as cards, what happens when no rule applies, its whole text |\n| **Tests** | the policy's tests, made of your agent's calls; the journal decided again |\n| **Run** | your agent's calls as they happen, each with its decision; a call that waits for you |\n| **Log** | every message, raw; the audit journal, downloaded or checked |\n| **Docs** | these pages |\n\n## 1. Connect your agent\n\nOpen **Tools**. The studio shows an MCP address for the chosen agent. Give it to your agent:\n\n```\nclaude mcp add --transport http okay-guard <the address>\n```\n\nIn other agents:\n\n| agent | how |\n|---|---|\n| **Codex** (CLI, IDE) | `codex mcp add okay-guard --url <the address>`, or in `~/.codex/config.toml`: a table `[mcp_servers.okay-guard]` with `url = \"<the address>\"`. `codex mcp list` shows it; `/mcp` in Codex shows its tools |\n| **Claude.ai, Claude Desktop** | Settings \u2192 Connectors \u2192 Add custom connector: the address |\n| **ChatGPT** | Settings \u2192 Apps & Connectors \u2192 Advanced settings: turn on Developer mode; then Create a connector with the address, no authentication, and turn it on in a chat |\n| **Cursor, VS Code, others** | an MCP server over HTTP (Streamable HTTP): the address |\n\nChatGPT and Claude.ai work in the cloud: they reach the address of the studio on the site\n(`https://okay-guard.okay-effects.workers.dev/m/\u2026/mcp`), not one on your machine (`http://127.0.0.1:\u2026`). Codex,\nClaude Code and Cursor run on your computer and reach either.\n\nKeep the studio's page open while the agent works: its calls are decided in the page. To take an agent away again:\n`claude mcp remove okay-guard`, in Codex its `remove` (`codex mcp --help`) or `enabled = false` in its table, or\nremove the connector in the app's settings.\n\nThe first agent works with a *simulated* payments server, so nothing real can happen. Try the prompt the studio\nsuggests (**Copy the prompt**).\n\n## 2. Watch and decide\n\nOpen **Run**. Each call shows its tool, its arguments and the decision. A call the policy asks about waits on its card:\n**Approve**, **Refuse**, or **Change** its arguments first. If no one answers in time, the agent is told it is not done.\n\n## 3. Write the policy\n\nUse **Make a rule** on a call's card, or open **Rules**:\n\n- each rule is a card; **Remove** takes it out;\n- **When no rule applies** says what happens then: allow, ask a person, or deny;\n- **A new rule** writes a rule from a form;\n- **Its text** is the whole policy, in the [policy language](policy.md).\n\n## 4. Add the tools you need\n\nIn **Tools**, add an MCP server by its URL, or a REST API by its OpenAPI: its operations become your agent's tools,\nand each call is decided by the policy before it is made. **Servers to try** has DeepWiki and Swagger's pet store; no\nkey is needed.\n\n## 5. Test it\n\nIn **Tests**, **Make tests of the calls seen** turns your agent's calls into tests: each call and the decision it got.\nThey run at every change of the policy. **Replay the journal** decides every recorded call again by the policy as it is\nnow, and shows what would change. See [tests](policy.md#tests).\n\n## 6. Take it to your machine\n\nIn **Tools**, **Export a copy** saves the agents, their servers and their policies in one file. Run okay-guard on your\nmachine and **Import a copy** there: the same policy decides, and every server is reachable. See [on your\nmachine](machine.md).\n"), $j_internal$002d767013ce0ee0f6d7a07587912eba3104cfaabc15.$m_sci_Nil$())))));
